{
  "_webhook_config": {
    "signature_location": "body",
    "signature_field": "bt_signature",
    "signature_algorithm": "braintree: hex(HMAC-SHA1(key = SHA1_raw_digest(private_key), message = bt_payload))",
    "signature_encoding": "hex",
    "webhook_secret_key": "braintree_webhook_fixture_secret_not_in_creds",
    "note": "Braintree puts its signature in the form BODY (bt_signature), not in a header, and the body is application/x-www-form-urlencoded rather than JSON. The harness's signature injector only writes into a header named by `signature_header` and only base64-encodes a body that is a JSON OBJECT, so neither mechanism can sign a Braintree payload. The bodies below are therefore already-base64-encoded literal strings of the full `bt_signature=...&bt_payload=...` form body, pre-signed. The keys are a fixture-local pair, not Braintree credentials: verification is a pure local HMAC that never contacts Braintree, so signing with a throwaway private key exercises the real algorithm end to end while keeping every real secret out of the repo. `webhook_secrets` is supplied inline for the same reason \u2014 the harness injects only `secret` from creds.json and Braintree also needs `additional_secret` (the PUBLIC key) to select its signature pair. `webhook_secret_key` deliberately names a key that does not exist in creds.json so that inline block is not overwritten.",
    "payload_source": "braintree_ruby lib/braintree/webhook_testing_gateway.rb \u2014 _transaction_settled_sample_xml / _transaction_settlement_declined_sample_xml, verbatim apart from the ids",
    "id_space_note": "<id> carries Braintree's LEGACY id (0gtq6gtd), which is what the gateway actually sends. UCS emits the Relay GLOBAL id (base64_nopad('transaction_' + legacy)) in every reference and response, because that is the id space the GraphQL Authorize/Refund legs stored."
  },
  "payment_succeeded": {
